#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100697 :question/id #uuid "9832238f-baff-5527-a11a-c65b6529cb0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4410" :question/problem "Calculate the product $C D,$ if\n![](images/94/945664c408577aac1d1a745120b3c17c46fd9db93e6d26cc53a4e501390936e2.png)\nand \n![](images/90/907ffa0b29d96249fe03a7fce3fe682c08982b14c7b7f72840540e6269196425.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3,$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/98/980ae75452824b4a8aa701b4e11be8c31448557933357458329f0a2b73a1cca6.png)\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ -1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a95370d-414b-5eac-8919-246de33eac09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099604} {:answer/id #uuid "a84bcb4e-521f-5ea1-9075-3e249dbed932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3.5 \\\\ -2.5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099605} {:answer/id #uuid "e006e13f-10ad-5b98-98da-0e4073b443c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.